The first watt ever measured in this repo, and it moves the headline finding: on the same
bf16 GEMM the NPU is **1.33x faster and 4.45x more energy-efficient**. Speed was never the
interesting half.
Desktop 2 (Ryzen 7 8700G / Phoenix XDNA1), DESKTOP-CBL5NUA, 2026-09-09, 22:08-22:11.
Four phases, ONE sitting, 40 s sampled per phase after a 3 s settle, `xrt-smi` clean
before and after.

WHY THIS EXISTS, AND WHY IT ALMOST DIDN'T
=========================================
docs/SILICON.md objective S4 and the backlog's energy study both need a watt, and this
repo had never measured one. The NPU exposes no power telemetry of its own: `xrt-smi`
reports `Estimated Power: N/A` and the electrical query fails at the driver escape
(0xc0000023).

The backlog's plan was to read HWiNFO64's shared-memory sensor export, on the stated
grounds that "HWiNFO64.INI already has SensorsSM=1, so the export is live". **That premise
is false.** `C:\Program Files\HWiNFO64\HWiNFO64.INI` contains five keys -- Theme,
SensorInterval, SensorsOnly, AutoUpdateBetaDisable, AutoUpdate -- and **no `SensorsSM`
line at all**, so the export is off and nothing can attach to `Global\HWiNFO_SENS_SM2`
(verified by probing the mapping, not by reading the INI alone). HWiNFO64 also runs
elevated here, so even with the export enabled a non-elevated reader would likely be
refused. Turning it on is a GUI checkbox or an admin INI edit plus restarting the user's
running instance -- a human action.

It turned out not to be needed. **Windows exposes AMD's RAPL counters through PDH
directly**, with no driver, no elevation, no HWiNFO and no hardware context:

    \Energy Meter(RAPL_Package0_PKG)\Power          package
    \Energy Meter(RAPL_Package0_CoreN_CORE)\Power   per core, N = 0..7
    \Energy Meter(_Total)\Power                     reads 0 on this machine, ignored

`tools/power_probe.py` samples them through the built-in `typeperf`.

THE UNIT IS MEASURED, NOT ASSUMED
=================================
`Power` is in MILLIWATTS, established by controlled burn rather than from documentation.
Against a 41.3 W idle, one busy thread steps the package **+9.59 W** and sixteen threads
step it **+42.1 W, to 83.5 W** -- which is where a 65 W-class Zen 4 desktop part belongs,
and the one-thread step is right for a single Zen 4 core at boost.

The same counter set exposes `Time` (~1007 units/s, i.e. milliseconds) and `Energy`.
**`Energy`'s absolute scale is UNRESOLVED** and is not used here: it matches neither
picojoules, nanojoules, microjoules nor millijoules against the Power reading. It does
track Power faithfully -- their ratio is constant at 0.0036 across a 2x power range, so
the two are consistent measurements of the same thing -- but an unexplained scale factor
is not something to integrate. This probe integrates `Power` and ignores `Energy`.

THE LIMIT THAT SHAPES EVERYTHING BELOW
======================================
**The NPU has no power domain of its own.** It shares the package with the CPU, so
`RAPL_Package0_PKG` includes it while the per-core meters do not. Every NPU figure here is
therefore a RESIDUAL -- package minus core-sum -- and is only meaningful against an idle
baseline AND a matched CPU-only control taken in the same sitting. That is S4's stated
method and it is why phases A and D exist. No bare wattage is quoted anywhere below.

WHAT WAS RUN
============
Identical work on both sides: bf16 GEMM at M=K=N=2048, i.e. 17.18 GFLOP per call. NPU is
the upstream `whole_array` design (4 columns, m=64/k=64/n=32, `--dtype_out f32`); CPU is
torch bf16 `a @ b`, 8 threads, both from the same ironenv process tree. Per-call times
were measured standalone, immediately before the power phases:

    CPU  14.436 ms/call   (693 calls in 10.00 s)  -> 1190.1 GFLOPS
    NPU  10.850 ms/call   (553 calls in  6.00 s)  -> 1583.4 GFLOPS

  python tools/power_probe.py --label A_idle       --duration 40
  python tools/power_probe.py --label B_cpu_gemm   --duration 40 --command "<cpu loop>"
  python tools/power_probe.py --label C_npu_gemm   --duration 40 --command "<npu loop>"
  python tools/power_probe.py --label D_idle_again --duration 40

RESULT
======
Raw phase means, 39 samples each:

  phase           package      cores sum   package - cores
  ---------------------------------------------------------
  A idle           42.060 W     26.194 W        15.866 W
  B cpu gemm       85.375 W     58.903 W        26.472 W
  C npu gemm       54.929 W     27.049 W        27.880 W
  D idle again     41.864 W     25.229 W        16.635 W

**The drift control passes:** A and D agree to 0.196 W on package (0.5%) and 0.97 W on
core-sum across a three-minute run, so the idle baseline is stable and the deltas below
are not riding a trend. Baseline used is the mean of A and D: 41.962 W package, 25.712 W
cores, 16.251 W residual.

Deltas over that baseline:

  workload    d package    d cores      d residual
  -------------------------------------------------
  CPU GEMM     43.413 W     33.191 W       10.221 W
  NPU GEMM     12.967 W      1.337 W       11.629 W

**This is the attribution, and it is clean.** During the NPU GEMM the cores barely move
(+1.34 W) while the residual jumps +11.63 W. During the CPU GEMM the opposite happens --
cores take +33.19 W and the residual moves +10.22 W (uncore and memory traffic serving
them). So the NPU's draw does land in this package domain, and it lands in the residual
rather than the cores, which is exactly where a separate accelerator on the same die
should appear. Had the package not moved while cores stayed flat, this measurement would
have been impossible and the item's premise would have failed a second time.

ENERGY PER GEMM
===============
Two framings, both reported, because they answer different questions.

  metric                             CPU        NPU      NPU advantage
  ----------------------------------------------------------------------
  time per GEMM                  14.436 ms   10.850 ms      1.33x
  throughput                     1190.1 GF   1583.4 GF      1.33x
  MARGINAL energy per GEMM       0.62671 J   0.14069 J     **4.45x**
  TOTAL package energy per GEMM  1.23247 J   0.59598 J       2.07x
  GFLOPS per marginal watt          27.4       122.1       **4.45x**
  GFLOPS per total package watt      13.9        28.8        2.07x

**Marginal** (delta over idle) is the honest number for "what does this workload cost the
machine", and it is where the NPU wins 4.45x. **Total** (whole package while running)
answers "what does the wall see", and the NPU still wins 2.07x -- less, because at these
power levels the ~42 W the machine burns at idle dominates, and that idle is itself
inflated by a peer process (see CONTENTION).

The shape of the finding matters more than either figure: **the NPU is barely faster and
dramatically cheaper.** A 1.33x speedup would be a marginal reason to use this hardware.
A 4.45x energy advantage at the same throughput is a different proposition, and it is the
first evidence in this repo for the thing NPUs are supposed to be for.

CONTENTION
==========
A peer process (PID 22396, `miniforge3\python.exe -`, running since 15:15) was live
throughout and took 24,647.2 -> 24,845.2 CPU-seconds across the run: 198 CPU-s over 199 s
of wall clock, i.e. **a steady ~1.0 core in every phase, including both idle phases**.

It inflates the idle baseline and it is present in all four phases, so the DELTAS still
isolate the measured workload -- which is precisely what a matched idle baseline is for.
The A-vs-D agreement (0.5% on package) is the evidence that its load was constant rather
than drifting. What it does bias is the TOTAL-energy column, by raising the idle floor
both workloads are charged for; the marginal column is unaffected. `xrt-smi` reported no
hardware contexts before or after, so nothing foreign touched the NPU.

WHAT THIS DOES NOT ESTABLISH
============================
- **One shape, one sitting, one dtype.** 2048^3 bf16 is a shape where the NPU already
  wins on throughput. At shapes where it loses on time (512^3, or anything under the
  dispatch floor) the energy verdict could invert, and nothing here tests that.
- **Not a per-frame or per-model figure.** This is joules per GEMM, not joules per
  inference. The backlog's J/frame study over real models is still open and is the point
  of building this instrument.
- **The CPU leg is torch bf16 at 8 threads**, the repo's established bf16 baseline, not a
  hand-tuned AVX-512 kernel and not all 16 threads. More threads would raise both
  throughput and power; which way the ratio moves is untested. Given how badly a
  single-implementation CPU baseline misled the activation sweep earlier today, treat the
  CPU column as one implementation's number rather than the CPU's ceiling.
- **The NPU residual is not purely the NPU.** It is uncore + SoC + NPU + memory
  controller. The +11.63 W is an upper bound on the NPU's own draw, not an isolate; the
  CPU phase's own +10.22 W residual shows how much of that term is memory traffic rather
  than compute.
- **No iGPU leg**, and no measurement of the idle NPU (whether an unused NPU costs
  anything at all).

